WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.558
Some invariants span multiple writes.

2
00:00:02.558 --> 00:00:04.803
Reserving inventory and recording its
reservation

3
00:00:04.803 --> 00:00:07.610
may need to succeed or fail together.

4
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.229
A database transaction groups
participating changes so

5
00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:12.474
a successful commit makes the unit

6
00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:15.374
durable, while rollback discards those
uncommitted changes.

7
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:17.245
Every participating statement must
actually

8
00:00:17.245 --> 00:00:19.303
use the same transaction context.

9
00:00:19.303 --> 00:00:23.981
Atomicity does not mean that all
transactions run one at a time.

10
00:00:23.981 --> 00:00:26.974
Isolation determines which concurrent
effects a transaction can

11
00:00:26.974 --> 00:00:30.529
observe and which conflicts the database
prevents or rejects.

12
00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:33.523
Placing ordinary reads and writes inside a
transaction

13
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:36.329
does not automatically protect every
business predicate.

14
00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:38.574
Two transactions may make incompatible
decisions

15
00:00:38.574 --> 00:00:41.474
from snapshots, including decisions
involving different rows.

16
00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:45.403
Verify the database's isolation guarantees
and the actual queries used.

17
00:00:45.403 --> 00:00:47.649
Pessimistic locking can protect a known

18
00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:50.081
resource before evaluating and modifying
it.

19
00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:54.384
Conflicting writers then wait or fail
according to the database's behavior.

20
00:00:54.384 --> 00:00:57.471
Lock the resource whose state determines
the decision,

21
00:00:57.471 --> 00:01:00.278
and hold protection until commit or
rollback.

22
00:01:00.278 --> 00:01:03.271
A row lock does not automatically protect
missing

23
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:07.107
rows, arbitrary ranges, related tables, or
an external service.

24
00:01:07.107 --> 00:01:09.446
Broader predicates may require
constraints, another

25
00:01:09.446 --> 00:01:11.597
coordination record, or serializable
isolation.

26
00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:13.655
Locks introduce contention and deadlocks.

27
00:01:13.655 --> 00:01:16.649
Acquire required locks in a consistent
order where

28
00:01:16.649 --> 00:01:20.017
possible, keep transactions focused, and
set sensible deadlines.

29
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:23.571
Avoid slow external network calls while
locks are held.

30
00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:26.565
A rollback in the database cannot undo a

31
00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:29.184
payment or email already accepted by
another

32
00:01:29.184 --> 00:01:32.459
system; those boundaries need a separate
reliable workflow.

33
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.704
Some databases reject conflicting
serializable transactions

34
00:01:34.704 --> 00:01:36.762
or choose a deadlock victim.

35
00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:39.755
Handle documented retryable failures by
rerunning the complete

36
00:01:39.755 --> 00:01:43.404
transaction from fresh reads, with bounded
attempts and backoff.

37
00:01:43.404 --> 00:01:45.275
Ensure repeated transaction execution
cannot

38
00:01:45.275 --> 00:01:47.333
duplicate effects outside that
transaction.

39
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:51.075
A process-local mutex is not a substitute
for these guarantees

40
00:01:51.075 --> 00:01:54.630
when other service instances can write the
same data.

41
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.372
Correctness comes from the selected
database semantics and all participating

42
00:01:58.372 --> 00:02:02.020
writers, not the presence of a transaction
function call.

